        x: 156;
        y: 250;
        width: 1600;
        height: 90;
        content: "A degree-$n$ polynomial $\\delta(s) = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 s + \\cdots + \\delta_n s^n$ is identified with its coefficient vector $\\underline{\\delta} = (\\delta_0, \\delta_1, \\dots, \\delta_n) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.";
        fontSize: 24;
        lineHeight: 1.45;
        color: "#24313f";
        math: true;
        animate {
            id: "a106";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "afterPrevious";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }

    code "e130" {
        x: 120;
        y: 380;
        width: 1680;
        height: 110;
        content: "/-- Coefficient vector `(α₀,…,αₙ) : ℝ^{n+1}` for `δ(s) = α₀+α₁s+…+αₙsⁿ`. -/\nabbrev CoeffVec (n : ℕ) := Fin (n + 1) → ℝ";
        language: "lean";
        theme: "light";
        fontSize: 17;
        animate {
            id: "a107";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "onClick";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }

    code "e131" {
        x: 120;
        y: 516;
        width: 1680;
        height: 170;
        content: "/-- Convert a coefficient vector α : Fin(n+1) → ℝ to a polynomial\n    δ(s) = α(0) + α(1)·s + ... + α(n)·sⁿ -/\nnoncomputable def polyOfVec {n : ℕ} (α : CoeffVec n) : Polynomial ℝ :=\n  ∑ j : Fin (n + 1), Polynomial.monomial j.val (α j)";
        language: "lean";
        theme: "light";
        fontSize: 17;
        animate {
            id: "a108";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "onClick";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }

    text "e132" {
        x: 120;
        y: 706;
        width: 1680;
        height: 80;
        content: "Example ($n = 2$). $\\texttt{CoeffVec}\\ 2 = \\texttt{Fin}\\ 3 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, i.e. $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Take $\\alpha = (1,-2,3)$, i.e. $\\alpha(0)=1$, $\\alpha(1)=-2$, $\\alpha(2)=3$. Then";
        fontSize: 24;
        color: "#24313f";
        math: true;
        animate {
            id: "a109";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "onClick";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }

    latex "e133" {
        x: 120;
        y: 796;
        width: 1680;
        height: 90;
        content: "\\texttt{polyOfVec}\\ \\alpha = 1 + (-2)\\,s + 3\\,s^2 = 3s^2 - 2s + 1";
        animate {
            id: "a110";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "afterPrevious";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }

    text "e134" {
        x: 120;
        y: 900;
        width: 1680;
        height: 70;
        content: "The coefficient vector uniquely determines the polynomial — and every polynomial of degree at most $n$ has exactly one coefficient vector in $\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.";
        fontSize: 22;
        lineHeight: 1.45;
        color: "#24313f";
        math: true;
        animate {
            id: "a111";
            effect: "fadeIn";
            trigger: "onClick";
            duration: 400;
            delay: 0;
            easing: "easeOut";
            direction: "up";
            repeat: 1;
        }
    }
}

